  intro: "Texas did not realign politically as a single statewide unit. Dallas-Fort Worth, Houston, Austin, San Antonio, East Texas, West Texas, the Panhandle, South Texas and the Gulf Coast moved on different schedules because their economies, settlement patterns, institutions and populations were different. Dallas became an early center of modern Republican organization; Houston combined energy conservatism with a large and increasingly diverse urban electorate; Austin evolved from a state-government and university city into the state's most consistently Democratic major metro core; San Antonio and South Texas retained distinct Democratic traditions; and rural regions moved toward the Republican Party at different speeds. The statewide shift is best understood as the accumulation of these regional realignments.",
